  "limitations": [
    "Screening aid, not a diagnosis. WakeSafe flags a pattern worth discussing with a clinician; it never tells a user they have sleep apnea.",
    "Trained on raw clinical polysomnography and ECG (UCD St Vincent's + PhysioNet Apnea-ECG), NOT on WHOOP nightly summaries. It validates the screening LOGIC on wearable-mappable signals; accuracy on real WHOOP data is not claimed and has not been measured. WHOOP computes SpO2, resting HR and HRV differently from these clinical sensors.",
    "Very small sample: a few overnight recordings per cohort (the full Apnea-ECG database could not be fetched in this environment). Leave-one-record-out validation is honest but confidence intervals are wide and a single unusual night moves the numbers.",
    "Per-minute labels within a night are correlated, so the effective sample size is far smaller than the minute count; held-out AUROC is reported per record so no single night hides in the pool.",
    "The two cohorts carry different raw signals (UCD has SpO2; Apnea-ECG does not), so they are validated separately rather than pooled -- pooling on the shared HR/HRV signals alone was weaker and would hide the fact that SpO2 is the dominant apnea signal.",
    "Output probability is NOT calibrated and must not be shown to a user as a percentage risk."
  ],
